00:55:36.130 --> 00:55:37.980
- It's a deal.
- It's a good deal.

00:55:37.980 --> 00:55:40.423
And it helps us, and, anyway.

00:55:41.910 --> 00:55:42.890
I'm not great at it.

00:55:42.890 --> 00:55:46.524
And we've talked about it in the past,

00:55:46.524 --> 00:55:47.700
and how we've, you know, you get into it.

00:55:47.700 --> 00:55:50.000
You have seasons of
being in and out of it,

00:55:50.000 --> 00:55:52.483
but whether you're actually learning

00:55:52.483 --> 00:55:55.550
and practicing mindfulness meditation,

00:55:55.550 --> 00:55:58.505
or if you're just taking
time to where it's like,

00:55:58.505 --> 00:55:59.980
you know what, I'm drinking my
tea, I'm drinking my coffee.

00:55:59.980 --> 00:56:01.740
I'm just gonna sit here.

00:56:01.740 --> 00:56:05.980
And I'm just going to notice myself.

00:56:05.980 --> 00:56:07.380
I'm gonna notice what it feels like.

00:56:07.380 --> 00:56:10.806
I'm gonna notice if I'm, you know,

00:56:10.806 --> 00:56:12.540
am I experiencing anxiety
or what, you know,

00:56:12.540 --> 00:56:13.560
whatever the case may be,

00:56:13.560 --> 00:56:17.813
and just give yourself freedom
to just not have to engage.

00:56:18.740 --> 00:56:20.200
With the way my brain works,

00:56:20.200 --> 00:56:24.040
I really do think it's
making a huge difference

00:56:24.040 --> 00:56:28.581
in my capacity to then go on with my life

00:56:28.581 --> 00:56:30.190
for the rest of the day.

00:56:30.190 --> 00:56:33.040
- Yeah, it's really tough for
me to do it in the morning.

00:56:34.454 --> 00:56:36.307
I think I've replaced it.

00:56:36.307 --> 00:56:39.621
And I know most of the time
I am listening to something,

00:56:39.621 --> 00:56:42.060
but like I'm getting that like half hour

00:56:42.060 --> 00:56:44.070
every, pretty much every day in the sauna.

00:56:44.070 --> 00:56:47.480
And I've thought about
making that into meditation,

00:56:47.480 --> 00:56:51.020
but it's almost the
heat is so intense that-

00:56:51.020 --> 00:56:52.403
- You need a distraction.

00:56:52.403 --> 00:56:53.810
- I need something to kind of focus on.

00:56:53.810 --> 00:56:55.337
But it is a very meditative time.

00:56:55.337 --> 00:56:56.950
And when I get out of the thing

00:56:56.950 --> 00:56:59.110
kind of in between them
and the pool, whatever,

00:56:59.110 --> 00:57:02.450
but I've been working out in the morning,

00:57:02.450 --> 00:57:05.440
and there was a time when I was meditating

00:57:05.440 --> 00:57:06.793
and then working out,

00:57:08.068 --> 00:57:10.004
and it's just like,

00:57:10.004 --> 00:57:12.800
I have to get up so early
in order to come to,

00:57:12.800 --> 00:57:14.183
my workout is like an hour.

00:57:15.040 --> 00:57:17.721
And so it's so tough, but.

00:57:17.721 --> 00:57:19.840
- But that's what it is for me.

00:57:19.840 --> 00:57:21.580
Are you saying, 'cause I was,

00:57:21.580 --> 00:57:24.710
I did want to just flat out ask,

00:57:24.710 --> 00:57:27.410
you know, what is the one practice

00:57:27.410 --> 00:57:29.560
that you would point to for you personally

00:57:29.560 --> 00:57:33.750
that is the key to your either success

00:57:33.750 --> 00:57:34.750
or personal wellbeing,

00:57:34.750 --> 00:57:38.020
if you were to nail it down to one thing?

00:57:38.020 --> 00:57:42.544
I think for me, it is that me-time.

00:57:42.544 --> 00:57:46.880
And then I think a corollary
is beginning to see exercise

00:57:46.880 --> 00:57:50.267
as something that allows me to,

00:57:50.267 --> 00:57:53.380
to get out of my head
and get into my body.

00:57:53.380 --> 00:57:56.190
And so it's not just
about all the other things

00:57:56.190 --> 00:57:59.166
you associate with working
out, but it's just about,

00:57:59.166 --> 00:58:04.166
it gives my brain a
break to actually engage

00:58:04.170 --> 00:58:07.080
in rigorous physical activity.

00:58:07.080 --> 00:58:08.760
So I think that's my answer.

00:58:08.760 --> 00:58:09.593
- Well,

00:58:10.460 --> 00:58:15.205
I mean, one thing before I give
you my answer is, you know,

00:58:15.205 --> 00:58:19.410
Eckhart Tolle talks about how mindfulness

00:58:19.410 --> 00:58:23.670
is kind of a misnomer, that
it's actually a mistranslation,

00:58:23.670 --> 00:58:28.360
because his whole deal is about
how the mind is the problem.

00:58:28.360 --> 00:58:29.490
You know what I'm saying?

00:58:29.490 --> 00:58:30.700
Like,

00:58:30.700 --> 00:58:35.700
thoughts and your mind feeling
like it has to be active

00:58:35.940 --> 00:58:39.160
and it has to be doing something
and identifying yourself

00:58:39.160 --> 00:58:43.490
with your mind is like the key
to all suffering essentially.

00:58:43.490 --> 00:58:45.200
Him does a much better
way of talking about it.

00:58:45.200 --> 00:58:48.770
He's also, his voice is very intoxicating,

00:58:48.770 --> 00:58:53.730
but so mindfulness is
actually about not thinking.

00:58:53.730 --> 00:58:57.250
Like, yeah, the reason that
you focus on your breath

00:58:57.250 --> 00:59:02.250
or you observe a thought is
actually so you won't think

00:59:02.520 --> 00:59:06.180
about those things and you
won't go down a rabbit trail.

00:59:06.180 --> 00:59:09.567
And that's why you keep
bringing your thoughts back

00:59:09.567 --> 00:59:12.780
to your breath, so you don't think.

00:59:12.780 --> 00:59:13.613
And I,

00:59:15.063 --> 00:59:18.480
and so, I mean, I find meditation
to be very helpful in that

00:59:18.480 --> 00:59:22.980
even though it's so difficult
for me to stop thinking.

00:59:22.980 --> 00:59:24.660
Because even when I do meditation

00:59:26.276 --> 00:59:31.276
I keep reframing it according
to how well I'm meditating,

00:59:31.660 --> 00:59:34.500
or, you know, it's like,
this is a very common problem

00:59:35.345 --> 00:59:36.620
for anybody trying to
gets into meditation.

00:59:36.620 --> 00:59:38.920
- If that makes you feel better.

00:59:38.920 --> 00:59:41.550
- And so, but what I found is that

00:59:43.554 --> 00:59:46.450
I have to because I'm forced
to because of my back.

00:59:46.450 --> 00:59:49.940
I have to every single
morning, I have to get up,

00:59:49.940 --> 00:59:50.860
and now I go down to,

00:59:50.860 --> 00:59:52.310
'cause we've kind of
redone the garage area,

00:59:52.310 --> 00:59:53.143
and it's kind of like a gym,

00:59:53.143 --> 00:59:55.130
and I've got like a yoga mat down there,

00:59:56.640 --> 00:59:59.830
I play some like peaceful music,

00:59:59.830 --> 01:00:01.730
some like Spotify meditation playlist.

01:00:03.750 --> 01:00:04.710
You know, I've got so many,

01:00:04.710 --> 01:00:06.617
and I know I keep promising
putting this on the site,

01:00:06.617 --> 01:00:11.617
and I will someday, but my stretching

01:00:11.670 --> 01:00:15.870
sort of yoga sort of back
essential routine every single day

01:00:15.870 --> 01:00:18.103
is about 20 minutes every single day.

01:00:19.390 --> 01:00:22.400
And so, and I'm not thinking
about anything at that point,

01:00:22.400 --> 01:00:25.860
other than what I'm doing to my body,

01:00:25.860 --> 01:00:27.453
you know what I'm saying?

01:00:27.453 --> 01:00:29.140
It's just like I'm stretching in this way.

01:00:29.140 --> 01:00:30.200
I'm stretching in this way.

01:00:30.200 --> 01:00:32.280
I'm doing this move. I'm doing that move.

01:00:32.280 --> 01:00:34.120
And I'm playing this music or whatever.

01:00:34.120 --> 01:00:36.460
So I think that's kind of
become my meditation time.

01:00:36.460 --> 01:00:39.510
And I have to do that in
order to stay healthy,

01:00:39.510 --> 01:00:41.350
but I also have to do that

01:00:41.350 --> 01:00:43.300
in order to then work out
without hurting myself.

01:00:43.300 --> 01:00:46.170
So it ends up being about an
hour and a half total time

01:00:47.100 --> 01:00:50.010
every day pretty much
of doing the, you know,

01:00:50.010 --> 01:00:52.606
half, 20 minutes or so of the stretching

01:00:52.606 --> 01:00:55.100
and then like a workout
that's about an hour long.

01:00:55.100 --> 01:00:59.140
I think that that's
become pretty essential,

01:00:59.140 --> 01:01:03.467
and it's become so, so much
of a rhythm in quarantine,

01:01:03.467 --> 01:01:05.490
that I'm kind of nervous

01:01:05.490 --> 01:01:07.620
about life going back
to the way that it was.

01:01:07.620 --> 01:01:10.850
I mean, a lot of people have
been talking about this lately.

01:01:10.850 --> 01:01:13.950
They're like, oh, now that life seems like

01:01:13.950 --> 01:01:15.420
it's about to return to normal,

01:01:15.420 --> 01:01:17.800
and, you know, everybody's
gonna be vaccinated.

01:01:17.800 --> 01:01:19.040
And then we're gonna
start doing all the things

01:01:19.040 --> 01:01:20.423
that we were doing before,

01:01:21.370 --> 01:01:23.950
there's this anxiety that a
lot of people are having about,

01:01:23.950 --> 01:01:25.500
some people is a social anxiety.

01:01:25.500 --> 01:01:27.130
Like I haven't been in a crowd.

01:01:27.130 --> 01:01:29.280
I'm kinda nervous about that.

01:01:29.280 --> 01:01:30.210
I don't, that's not me.

01:01:30.210 --> 01:01:31.410
That's not how I think about it.

01:01:31.410 --> 01:01:32.830
I think I have an anxiety

01:01:33.674 --> 01:01:37.447
about being pulled out of these rhythms

01:01:39.410 --> 01:01:41.770
that I've been able to establish,

01:01:41.770 --> 01:01:45.630
that like, yeah, okay, if
we go to New York City,

01:01:45.630 --> 01:01:49.738
I can do the back routine
on the hotel floor, right?

01:01:49.738 --> 01:01:52.045
But if you want to work out, you're like,

01:01:52.045 --> 01:01:53.320
well, I'm gonna go to the hotel gym

01:01:53.320 --> 01:01:55.050
and what are they gonna have,

01:01:55.050 --> 01:01:55.883
and, oh, what am I gonna do at night?

01:01:55.883 --> 01:01:58.608
I can't go, I can't go get my sauna.

01:01:58.608 --> 01:01:59.441
You know what I'm saying?

01:01:59.441 --> 01:02:01.140
It's like, these are privileged problems,

01:02:01.140 --> 01:02:05.130
but I just have this anxiety about not,

01:02:05.130 --> 01:02:09.063
this has become such a part
of my existence at this point.

01:02:09.960 --> 01:02:13.910
I haven't thought about it in
these terms, in this non-time.

01:02:13.910 --> 01:02:15.400
But I think it's probably
one of the reasons

01:02:15.400 --> 01:02:17.640
that I feel good right now.

01:02:17.640 --> 01:02:19.440
I feel kind of healthy
because I've got these things

01:02:19.440 --> 01:02:20.730
that I'm doing almost every single day

01:02:20.730 --> 01:02:21.880
that are pretty consistent

01:02:21.880 --> 01:02:24.523
and not focused on doing,
doing, doing, doing.

01:02:25.424 --> 01:02:26.550
- I think that's why now is a good time

01:02:26.550 --> 01:02:30.950
to have this conversation because
as things start to change,

01:02:30.950 --> 01:02:34.030
it's like, okay, is there
something new to incorporate

01:02:34.030 --> 01:02:38.307
or something that you started
to incorporate that you want,

01:02:38.307 --> 01:02:41.270
that there's a new priority
associated with it,

01:02:41.270 --> 01:02:43.643
that you're gonna protect that, you know?

01:02:46.555 --> 01:02:47.890
I hope that's true

01:02:48.890 --> 01:02:53.890
of me, that I'll keep getting
up and taking that time.

01:02:54.800 --> 01:02:58.300
- Well, I mean, the good
news for me is the fact

01:02:58.300 --> 01:03:02.313
that I have, like, if I
don't do the stretching,

01:03:03.210 --> 01:03:06.261
I literally can't go on about my day.

01:03:06.261 --> 01:03:07.690
Like I will, I'll be in pain
for the rest of the day.

01:03:07.690 --> 01:03:11.730
So that's kind of the blessing
of a lower back injury

01:03:11.730 --> 01:03:14.550
or chronic pain or whatever it is.

01:03:14.550 --> 01:03:17.540
Is that, okay, like if we're
traveling, and we're like,

01:03:17.540 --> 01:03:19.470
okay, we gotta be at this
interview at seven o'clock,

01:03:19.470 --> 01:03:21.266
